1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It pulls and stretches, bubbles and hardens,

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
crunches and melts.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
And it can get you out of the doghouse.

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
Sweet treats produced by robots, extruders,

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
enrovers and pullers.

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Some nutty, some chocolate covered,

7
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:21,000
some even full of bugs.

8
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Americans consume about 7 billion pounds each year.

9
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, Candy on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:46,360 --> 00:00:49,060
Nearly $25 billion worth of candy

11
00:00:49,160 --> 00:00:52,460
is sold every year in the US.

12
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:55,000
An energy-boosting snack on the run,

13
00:00:55,001 --> 00:00:57,660
or a piece of art to be savored.

14
00:00:57,760 --> 00:01:00,300
Candy is for indulging.

15
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:02,040
The busiest time in any candy store

16
00:01:02,140 --> 00:01:05,240
is always holiday time, when we tend to indulge just a little

17
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:06,140
more.

18
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:10,780
Jelly beans for Easter, conversation hearts

19
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:13,820
for Valentine's Day, a variety of small candies

20
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,860
for Halloween, and candy canes at Christmas.

21
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:24,360
Chocolate represents more than 60% of all the candy sold in the

22
00:01:24,361 --> 00:01:25,700
in the US.

23
00:01:25,800 --> 00:01:28,100
And nowhere is that more apparent than at the Hershey

24
00:01:28,200 --> 00:01:30,900
Company, headquartered where else?

25
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,940
Hershey, Pennsylvania, a town dedicated to all things

26
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:35,000
chocolate.

27
00:01:35,100 --> 00:01:37,940
The residential streets are all named after cocoa growing

28
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:39,200
regions in the world.

29
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:42,520
So there are streets like Caracas Avenue, and Arriba

30
00:01:42,620 --> 00:01:47,720
Avenue, and Java Avenue, and Trinidad Avenue, so that we

31
00:01:47,820 --> 00:01:52,620
never forget what made this town possible.

32
00:01:52,621 --> 00:01:56,620
The man who made it all possible was Milton Hershey.

33
00:01:56,621 --> 00:02:01,260
At the 1893 Chicago World's Fair, he purchased German-developed

34
00:02:01,261 --> 00:02:04,260
chocolate-making machinery and soon began producing bars in

35
00:02:04,261 --> 00:02:07,860
Lancaster, Pennsylvania. A bar's price?

36
00:02:07,861 --> 00:02:12,580
Five cents. Over a century later,

37
00:02:12,581 --> 00:02:16,180
Hershey is North America's largest producer of candy,

38
00:02:16,181 --> 00:02:20,780
mostly of the chocolate variety, with more than 13,000 employees

39
00:02:20,781 --> 00:02:23,280
and annual revenues of over $4 billion.

40
00:02:26,280 --> 00:02:30,620
The company uses 600 million pounds of chocolate annually,

41
00:02:30,621 --> 00:02:34,860
as well as over 200 million pounds of nuts, over 700

42
00:02:34,861 --> 00:02:38,860
million pounds of milk, and an undisclosed amount of sugar.

43
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:43,360
But since it's the first ingredient listed in milk

44
00:02:43,361 --> 00:02:46,240
chocolate, you can be certain that it exceeds the amount of

45
00:02:46,241 --> 00:02:47,400
any other ingredient.

46
00:02:47,401 --> 00:02:50,100
ingredient.

47
00:02:50,200 --> 00:02:52,800
Despite Hershey's seemingly endless line of chocolate

48
00:02:52,900 --> 00:02:57,740
products, they all share a common starting point.

49
00:02:57,840 --> 00:03:00,540
At cocoa plantations around the world,

50
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:06,160
workers split open cocoa pods to remove the beans and pulp.

51
00:03:06,260 --> 00:03:08,720
Later, the beans and pulp will be laid out for approximately

52
00:03:08,820 --> 00:03:10,160
six days to ferment.

53
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:15,460
During the fermentation process, yeast

54
00:03:15,461 --> 00:03:18,900
The yeast metabolizes the sugars in the pulp, producing ethanol

55
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:23,360
as a byproduct. Bacteria consume this ethanol

56
00:03:23,460 --> 00:03:26,300
and produce lactic and acetic acids.

57
00:03:26,400 --> 00:03:29,500
This reaction allows the cocoa flavor of the beans to develop

58
00:03:29,600 --> 00:03:32,220
and some of the bitterness to subside.

59
00:03:36,520 --> 00:03:38,820
After drying in the sun, the beans are shipped to chocolate

60
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:40,960
producers, like Hershey.

61
00:03:40,961 --> 00:03:45,960
Here the beans are cleaned, roasted, shelled, and ground

62
00:03:46,060 --> 00:03:50,600
into a chocolate liquor. This chocolate liquor is blended

63
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:55,200
with milk solids and sweeteners and sent for refining.

64
00:03:55,300 --> 00:03:58,320
Milk chocolate contains at least 10% chocolate liquor

65
00:03:58,420 --> 00:04:02,520
and 12% milk solids. The product that you see on

66
00:04:02,620 --> 00:04:05,660
the belt is our sweetened condensed milk, which has the

67
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:10,960
fluid milk sugar that has been sent up to our drying operation

68
00:04:10,961 --> 00:04:13,860
We have added chocolate liquor and cocoa butter to it,

69
00:04:13,960 --> 00:04:16,500
and we are now refining that product into the crumb

70
00:04:16,600 --> 00:04:19,500
that you see on the belt.

71
00:04:19,600 --> 00:04:22,800
This chocolate crumb enters the conch, essentially a giant

72
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:26,640
mixing bowl where alternating rollers incorporate soy lecithin

73
00:04:26,740 --> 00:04:31,080
and additional cocoa butter to make a chocolate paste, which

74
00:04:31,180 --> 00:04:34,520
will be used in Hershey's milk chocolate bars and Hershey's

75
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:37,180
Kisses.

76
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:40,520
Behind me is the milk chocolate bar line.

77
00:04:40,521 --> 00:04:44,020
And in front of me is the almond chocolate bar line.

78
00:04:44,021 --> 00:04:49,020
These two lines were recently modernized and brought to this facility.

79
00:04:49,021 --> 00:04:55,020
You can see there's milk chocolate and it is going through a cleaner and then it will be tempered.

80
00:04:55,021 --> 00:05:00,020
And during the tempering process, the chocolate is heated and cooled

81
00:05:00,021 --> 00:05:04,020
and it's time to get the right cocoa butter crystal structure.

82
00:05:04,021 --> 00:05:10,020
From a consumer standpoint, what that means is it will have a good shine, it will be hard,

83
00:05:10,021 --> 00:05:12,920
that won't melt in your hands, and the occurrence

84
00:05:12,921 --> 00:05:16,020
will last longer.

85
00:05:16,021 --> 00:05:18,760
To form the bars, machines deposit measured loads

86
00:05:18,761 --> 00:05:22,200
of milk chocolate into the trays.

87
00:05:22,201 --> 00:05:26,440
100% chocolate on the plain bar line, 82% chocolate,

88
00:05:26,441 --> 00:05:31,740
and 18% roasted almonds on the almond bar line.

89
00:05:31,741 --> 00:05:34,940
Both lines travel through a 45 degree cooling tunnel

90
00:05:34,941 --> 00:05:38,640
to solidify the bars.

91
00:05:38,641 --> 00:05:41,760
As a safety precaution, the bars pass through a metal detector,

92
00:05:42,400 --> 00:05:45,800
necessary because much of the production line is made of stainless steel

93
00:05:45,801 --> 00:05:50,240
and other metal components, and the detector guards against the rare instance

94
00:05:50,241 --> 00:05:52,200
of metal finding its way into the bars.

95
00:05:55,760 --> 00:05:57,560
And then it's time to wrap it up.

96
00:05:57,561 --> 00:06:04,720
We have a wrapping machine and a robot that actually packs boxes or trays

97
00:06:04,721 --> 00:06:06,120
from those top of bars.

98
00:06:06,121 --> 00:06:11,120
This operation was modernized from an all-hand-packed line,

99
00:06:11,220 --> 00:06:13,620
which produced at most about a third

100
00:06:13,720 --> 00:06:15,620
of what this line is capable of.

101
00:06:15,720 --> 00:06:20,660
These rolls of wrap are about 10,000 bars on a roll.

102
00:06:20,760 --> 00:06:24,880
We change them every 20 minutes on each machine.

103
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:29,980
We make about 1.9 million bars a day off of these lines.

104
00:06:32,580 --> 00:06:35,080
Beyond the bars are the kisses.

105
00:06:35,081 --> 00:06:36,840
But even these old-time favorites

106
00:06:36,940 --> 00:06:39,720
have leapt into the 21st century.

107
00:06:39,820 --> 00:06:42,780
For over 90 years, kisses were made

108
00:06:42,880 --> 00:06:46,320
by depositing chocolate onto a conveyor belt.

109
00:06:46,420 --> 00:06:48,820
But with the recent technology, we

110
00:06:48,920 --> 00:06:55,040
are actually able to make several varieties of new kisses.

111
00:06:55,140 --> 00:06:57,340
We've made some filled kisses.

112
00:06:57,440 --> 00:07:03,340
We deposit caramel or cherry filling in the center.

113
00:07:03,341 --> 00:07:08,640
These kisses are then wrapped individually at about 1,000

114
00:07:08,740 --> 00:07:10,080
a minute.

115
00:07:10,180 --> 00:07:12,920
And between the two kiss lines we have here,

116
00:07:13,020 --> 00:07:18,680
we make 500,000 kisses an hour, or over 10 million kisses a day.

117
00:07:18,780 --> 00:07:22,160
So that's a lot of love.

118
00:07:22,260 --> 00:07:27,400
And the source of all this love is the cocoa tree.

119
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:30,600
It really started in what today would be southern Mexico

120
00:07:30,700 --> 00:07:32,640
and northern South America.

121
00:07:32,641 --> 00:07:36,080
and it grows wild in the rainforest.

122
00:07:36,180 --> 00:07:38,740
And it needs a lot of rain, it needs a lot of sun,

123
00:07:38,840 --> 00:07:42,380
and it needs a lot of shade.

124
00:07:42,480 --> 00:07:46,280
And chocolate must grow within 20 degrees of the equator.

125
00:07:46,380 --> 00:07:50,300
Nowadays, most of the cocoa is grown in Africa, in South

126
00:07:50,400 --> 00:07:54,300
America, and in Indonesia.

127
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:57,040
In the early 1500s, the Aztec emperor

128
00:07:57,140 --> 00:08:02,640
Montezuma supposedly drank over 50 goblets a day of chocolatl.

129
00:08:02,641 --> 00:08:04,840
a beverage made of ground-roasted cocoa beans,

130
00:08:04,940 --> 00:08:07,240
water, and spices.

131
00:08:07,340 --> 00:08:10,840
When the Spanish explorer Cortez went to Mexico,

132
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:14,080
he met with Montezuma, and there was offered

133
00:08:14,180 --> 00:08:15,680
a chocolate beverage.

134
00:08:15,780 --> 00:08:19,120
And he was so taken by it, he brought it back to Spain,

135
00:08:19,220 --> 00:08:22,220
and it became the drink of royalty.

136
00:08:22,320 --> 00:08:25,160
And the Spanish liked it so much that they kept it to themselves

137
00:08:25,260 --> 00:08:27,760
for almost 100 years, but you couldn't keep something

138
00:08:27,860 --> 00:08:29,060
like that a secret.

139
00:08:29,160 --> 00:08:32,200
And so it slowly spread across Europe.

140
00:08:32,201 --> 00:08:34,060
But it was consumed as a beverage

141
00:08:34,160 --> 00:08:37,040
for the next several hundred years.

142
00:08:37,140 --> 00:08:41,740
In 1828, Dutch chocolate maker C.J. Van Houten

143
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:44,540
patented this chocolate press that would revolutionize

144
00:08:44,640 --> 00:08:46,540
the industry.

145
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:49,320
Van Houten's machine, like this newer version,

146
00:08:49,420 --> 00:08:51,960
pressed about half of the naturally occurring fat out

147
00:08:52,060 --> 00:08:54,920
of the roasted cocoa bean.

148
00:08:55,020 --> 00:08:57,100
This resulted in a drier cake that

149
00:08:57,200 --> 00:08:59,560
could be pulverized into a fine powder

150
00:08:59,561 --> 00:09:02,660
with additional cocoa butter to achieve a certain smooth

151
00:09:02,760 --> 00:09:03,560
consistency.

152
00:09:05,700 --> 00:09:08,800
Van Houten's viscous chocolate, just like the chocolate produced

153
00:09:08,900 --> 00:09:12,440
now, could be poured into molds and solidified into bars for

154
00:09:12,540 --> 00:09:15,080
the first time.

155
00:09:15,180 --> 00:09:17,980
The rest is history.

156
00:09:18,080 --> 00:09:21,920
Chocolate bars like Hershey's are big business, but for special

157
00:09:22,020 --> 00:09:26,860
occasions, it's the boxed chocolates that reigns supreme.

158
00:09:26,861 --> 00:09:32,460
And here at C's Candies, they make over 100 kinds.

159
00:09:32,540 --> 00:09:36,860
Dark or milk chocolate, bonbons, nuts and chews,

160
00:09:36,940 --> 00:09:42,540
something for every taste, geared toward adult sensibilities.

161
00:09:42,600 --> 00:09:48,620
C's was started in Los Angeles in 1921 by a couple of Canadians,

162
00:09:48,680 --> 00:09:51,080
Charles C. and his mother Mary C.

163
00:09:51,160 --> 00:09:55,560
And Mary C.'s recipes were what he actually started the business

164
00:09:55,620 --> 00:09:56,860
on.

165
00:09:56,861 --> 00:10:00,660
Seas offers traditional boxed candy with some help from

166
00:10:00,760 --> 00:10:02,760
mechanization to pump up the volume.

167
00:10:06,300 --> 00:10:09,600
Raw ingredients are brought in by the truckload and stored in

168
00:10:09,700 --> 00:10:12,400
silos until they're ready for use.

169
00:10:12,500 --> 00:10:17,420
This department here makes the caramel for the rum nougat.

170
00:10:17,520 --> 00:10:23,360
And the caramel portion is made up of sugar, corn syrup, and 36%

171
00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:24,360
cream.

172
00:10:24,361 --> 00:10:30,360
We cook it on the open fire in batches of approximately 75 pounds each.

173
00:10:30,361 --> 00:10:34,360
The caramel gets cooked to roughly 250 degrees.

174
00:10:34,361 --> 00:10:38,360
And at this stage, we're probably about five minutes into the cook.

175
00:10:38,361 --> 00:10:42,360
It takes approximately 20 minutes to complete a cook.

176
00:10:42,361 --> 00:10:46,360
And as it's cooking, you can see how it gets progressively browner.

177
00:10:46,361 --> 00:10:49,360
That's the caramelizing process.

178
00:10:49,361 --> 00:10:52,360
And this one here is ready to come off the fire.

179
00:10:54,360 --> 00:10:56,360
is brought over to the giant mixer

180
00:10:56,361 --> 00:10:59,360
and combined with cherries and maceta,

181
00:10:59,361 --> 00:11:03,360
a sweetened, beaten egg white mixture.

182
00:11:03,361 --> 00:11:06,360
That'll be mixed for approximately five minutes,

183
00:11:06,361 --> 00:11:10,360
and then we'll progressively add raisins, walnuts,

184
00:11:10,361 --> 00:11:11,360
and flavor.

185
00:11:11,361 --> 00:11:13,360
After that's all blended together,

186
00:11:13,361 --> 00:11:18,360
that'll be the center for C's famous rum nougat.

187
00:11:18,361 --> 00:11:21,360
The mixture is poured into trays, cooled,

188
00:11:21,361 --> 00:11:25,100
and sent to enrobing for its chocolate bath.

189
00:11:25,200 --> 00:11:27,100
This is our enrobing department.

190
00:11:27,200 --> 00:11:31,200
We have five enrobing lines, and each one

191
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:35,400
is approximately 300 feet long and takes 30 minutes

192
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:40,520
for the time we put them on and take them off the other end.

193
00:11:40,620 --> 00:11:43,740
The process begins as candy handlers visually inspect

194
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:46,220
each piece and place it on the conveyor belt.

195
00:11:48,720 --> 00:11:51,100
Along the candy's 300-foot long

196
00:11:51,101 --> 00:11:54,700
it gets an initial application of chocolate,

197
00:11:54,800 --> 00:11:56,900
a blast of air to remove excess chocolate

198
00:11:57,000 --> 00:12:00,840
and leave a rippled finish, and finally a second coat

199
00:12:00,940 --> 00:12:01,840
for good measure.

200
00:12:05,280 --> 00:12:08,780
Chocolate may be king, it sees, but the company also

201
00:12:08,880 --> 00:12:11,820
makes a killer peanut brittle with help

202
00:12:11,920 --> 00:12:13,720
from some very animated robots.

203
00:12:17,120 --> 00:12:20,620
It all starts with a mixture of peanuts, butter, corn syrup,

204
00:12:20,621 --> 00:12:22,620
sugar, and salt.

205
00:12:22,621 --> 00:12:27,620
800 pounds per hour are cooked to just over 300 degrees.

206
00:12:27,621 --> 00:12:30,620
After it's cooked, we need to cool it with cold water

207
00:12:30,621 --> 00:12:33,620
at 40 degrees, and it starts to cool the bottom of it.

208
00:12:33,621 --> 00:12:38,620
We have a top roller which flattens it, cools the top.

209
00:12:38,621 --> 00:12:42,620
The continuous sheet then moves down to a robot that cuts it

210
00:12:42,621 --> 00:12:45,620
and flips it, and the employees here have called it

211
00:12:45,621 --> 00:12:47,620
Tweety Bird.

212
00:12:47,621 --> 00:12:52,620
Tweedy is a six-axis robot that can simulate all the movements

213
00:12:52,621 --> 00:12:54,860
of a human arm, elbow, and wrist.

214
00:12:54,861 --> 00:12:58,000
It took over the monotonous task of flipping and cutting peanut

215
00:12:58,001 --> 00:13:00,800
brittle in 2003.

216
00:13:00,801 --> 00:13:04,100
After the cutting and flipping, it moves into another station.

217
00:13:04,101 --> 00:13:07,600
It's continually cooling, and it's spread.

218
00:13:07,601 --> 00:13:10,680
And we're using a robot for that as well,

219
00:13:10,681 --> 00:13:14,620
and it's called Sylvester.

220
00:13:14,621 --> 00:13:17,620
Then guess what?

221
00:13:17,621 --> 00:13:20,280
More chocolate.

222
00:13:20,360 --> 00:13:23,960
Seas uses about 10 million pounds of chocolate every year.

223
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:28,860
Impressive, but only a drop in a very big bucket.

224
00:13:28,940 --> 00:13:31,240
It may be the lion's share of the industry,

225
00:13:31,300 --> 00:13:35,380
but there's more to candy than mass-produced chocolate.

226
00:13:35,440 --> 00:13:38,340
For a growing number of sweet tooth traditionalists,

227
00:13:38,400 --> 00:13:44,080
the perfect sweet is a small handmade treat.

228
00:13:44,140 --> 00:13:47,080
If laid end to end, the Hershey's Chocolate

229
00:13:47,081 --> 00:13:50,420
produced in one year would circle the earth two and a

230
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:51,520
half times.

231
00:14:01,020 --> 00:14:04,440
While places like Hershey and C's crank out thousands of pounds

232
00:14:04,500 --> 00:14:10,240
of chocolate every day, some candy makers believe that less

233
00:14:10,300 --> 00:14:13,640
really is more.

234
00:14:13,700 --> 00:14:15,900
That's the mantra at Schimpf's Confectionary.

235
00:14:15,901 --> 00:14:19,600
in Jeffersonville, Indiana, a mom and pop candy shop,

236
00:14:19,700 --> 00:14:22,300
complete with lovely young ladies hand-dipping chocolates

237
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:24,780
for all to see.

238
00:14:24,880 --> 00:14:27,540
Oh, I've had a lot of offers for my hand.

239
00:14:27,640 --> 00:14:29,920
And then always, I have people to ask me

240
00:14:30,020 --> 00:14:31,520
if I lick my fingers.

241
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:33,180
And of course, I have to tell them no,

242
00:14:33,280 --> 00:14:35,560
since I'm in the window.

243
00:14:35,660 --> 00:14:38,420
Gene's been doing this job for over 50 years

244
00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:41,860
and considers it a true art form.

245
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:45,900
The earliest versions of candy were anything but art.

246
00:14:45,901 --> 00:14:48,740
It was strictly about satisfying man's natural yearning for

247
00:14:48,840 --> 00:14:51,680
something sweet.

248
00:14:51,780 --> 00:14:55,240
Ancient cultures preserved nuts, fruits, and even flowers and

249
00:14:55,340 --> 00:15:00,280
honey to satiate their sweet teeth.

250
00:15:00,380 --> 00:15:04,660
As early as 500 BC, sugar cane growers in India were extracting

251
00:15:04,760 --> 00:15:09,260
the reed's juice and boiling it, yielding sucrose, sugar in a

252
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:12,220
crystalline form used to sweeten food and drink.

253
00:15:15,900 --> 00:15:18,500
By the 11th century, this practice had traveled west

254
00:15:18,600 --> 00:15:21,140
to the Holy Land, where European crusaders

255
00:15:21,240 --> 00:15:24,040
got their first taste.

256
00:15:24,140 --> 00:15:26,380
And they liked it, enough to experiment

257
00:15:26,480 --> 00:15:28,480
with ways to harness that sweet flavor

258
00:15:28,580 --> 00:15:31,880
into portable bits of decadence, candy.

259
00:15:31,980 --> 00:15:35,080
The early candies were primarily what they call

260
00:15:35,180 --> 00:15:39,220
boiled candy, so there'd be lemon drops and peppermints,

261
00:15:39,320 --> 00:15:42,920
and even some early lollipops, licorice.

262
00:15:43,020 --> 00:15:45,660
Those sort of candies were some of the first candies

263
00:15:45,661 --> 00:15:49,160
consumed. Boiling sucrose with water

264
00:15:49,260 --> 00:15:52,500
in a kettle created a product that was not only delicious,

265
00:15:52,600 --> 00:15:55,300
but easy to manipulate into many variations,

266
00:15:55,400 --> 00:15:58,000
depending on the flavors added and the cooking temperature.

267
00:16:01,100 --> 00:16:03,780
More heat causes more water to evaporate.

268
00:16:03,880 --> 00:16:08,120
Less water means higher sugar concentration.

269
00:16:08,220 --> 00:16:13,220
Lower temperatures make chewy candy, like fudge and taffy.

270
00:16:13,221 --> 00:16:16,660
Mid-range temperatures make soft candy, like nougat

271
00:16:16,720 --> 00:16:18,620
and gummies.

272
00:16:18,700 --> 00:16:21,960
And hotter temperatures make hard candy, like candy canes

273
00:16:22,020 --> 00:16:22,860
and lollipops.

274
00:16:25,400 --> 00:16:27,660
Sugar cane didn't grow well at home,

275
00:16:27,740 --> 00:16:31,900
so Europeans started plantations in their Caribbean colonies.

276
00:16:31,980 --> 00:16:34,280
But importing the crop wasn't cheap,

277
00:16:34,340 --> 00:16:37,920
and it was mostly the elite who could indulge in sweets.

278
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:41,320
In 18th century Europe, scientists discovered that the

279
00:16:41,321 --> 00:16:43,520
that the less climate-finicky sugar beet

280
00:16:43,521 --> 00:16:45,620
could be grown locally and processed

281
00:16:45,621 --> 00:16:50,960
into the same sucrose. This practice traveled

282
00:16:50,961 --> 00:16:54,260
to North America, and combined with the advent of molding

283
00:16:54,261 --> 00:16:57,560
machines made by companies like Thomas Mills and Brother,

284
00:16:57,561 --> 00:17:01,480
it made candy accessible to everyone.

285
00:17:01,481 --> 00:17:06,680
And by the mid-1800s, there were over 380 candy factories

286
00:17:06,681 --> 00:17:09,620
in the US. Advertisements

287
00:17:09,621 --> 00:17:13,220
sprung up everywhere. Penny candies were sold at drug

288
00:17:13,221 --> 00:17:17,220
stores. This is precisely the kind of

289
00:17:17,221 --> 00:17:21,860
stuff you'll find at Schimp's. Despite the amazing advances in

290
00:17:21,861 --> 00:17:24,820
technology and mass production, the Schimp family has been making

291
00:17:24,821 --> 00:17:29,780
candy the old-fashioned way since 1891.

292
00:17:29,781 --> 00:17:33,780
And their loyal customers wouldn't have it any other way.

293
00:17:33,781 --> 00:17:36,420
Just ask store owners Jill and Warren Schimp.

294
00:17:36,421 --> 00:17:37,960
in the shape of hearts.

295
00:17:37,961 --> 00:17:40,320
One of their biggest sellers is Red Hots.

296
00:17:40,321 --> 00:17:45,020
We have corn syrup in the pot.

297
00:17:45,021 --> 00:17:50,860
We're going to add a little water to dissolve the corn syrup.

298
00:17:50,861 --> 00:17:54,080
Turn the heat on, and then we're going to add the sugar.

299
00:17:59,080 --> 00:18:01,480
More water is added to dissolve the sugar,

300
00:18:01,481 --> 00:18:05,220
and the mixture is brought to a boil.

301
00:18:05,221 --> 00:18:08,920
Next, add the red food coloring and continue cooking for about 20

302
00:18:09,020 --> 00:18:14,100
minutes to 320 degrees. The red hot brew is then poured

303
00:18:14,200 --> 00:18:18,160
onto a table with a temperature controlled surface.

304
00:18:18,260 --> 00:18:21,000
Different candies need to be at different temperatures to be

305
00:18:21,100 --> 00:18:24,440
manipulated as needed, so this table is every traditional

306
00:18:24,540 --> 00:18:31,440
candy maker's best friend. The water jacketed table has a

307
00:18:31,441 --> 00:18:37,120
has a steel top with a flat pan or trough attached underneath.

308
00:18:37,180 --> 00:18:40,480
A handle turns on either the hot or cold water, which is

309
00:18:40,540 --> 00:18:42,980
distributed evenly in the trough.

310
00:18:43,060 --> 00:18:45,320
Hot water heats the metal top.

311
00:18:45,380 --> 00:18:48,120
Cold water draws heat away, cooling the tabletop.

312
00:18:51,900 --> 00:18:56,600
The red hot candy needs to be cooled so it can harden.

313
00:18:56,660 --> 00:18:59,540
But while Schimpf's signature red hot cinnamon candy is still

314
00:18:59,541 --> 00:19:02,540
hot, it isn't cinnamon hot just yet.

315
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:05,080
This is our cinnamon oil.

316
00:19:05,180 --> 00:19:08,080
We add a small amount of cinnamon.

317
00:19:08,180 --> 00:19:11,980
Cinnamon comes from the bark of a tree, which is native to China.

318
00:19:12,080 --> 00:19:14,980
It comes a long way to be in our red hots here in Indiana.

319
00:19:18,300 --> 00:19:20,700
Once the candy is brought down to a more manageable

320
00:19:20,800 --> 00:19:23,240
temperature, it's ready to be molded into any shape

321
00:19:23,340 --> 00:19:24,500
the shimps want.

322
00:19:24,501 --> 00:19:28,000
want.

323
00:19:28,100 --> 00:19:32,200
Their collection of specialty dyes is vast.

324
00:19:32,300 --> 00:19:35,180
So a lump of warm, malleable candy goes in,

325
00:19:35,280 --> 00:19:38,680
shiny red cinnamon drops come out.

326
00:19:38,780 --> 00:19:42,060
Or a lump of warm, malleable candy goes in,

327
00:19:42,160 --> 00:19:45,980
shiny red cinnamon hearts come out, and so on.

328
00:19:46,080 --> 00:19:47,860
Originally, this machine was hand cranked,

329
00:19:47,960 --> 00:19:51,120
and we would have cranked the candy through by hand.

330
00:19:51,220 --> 00:19:54,100
We put the motors in the motor drive on in the 1980s,

331
00:19:54,101 --> 00:19:56,740
and that's when we said we joined the 20th century.

332
00:19:56,840 --> 00:19:59,340
We really don't plan on joining the 21st century

333
00:19:59,440 --> 00:20:03,340
because we enjoy demonstrating here at Chimps the way candy

334
00:20:03,440 --> 00:20:06,840
has been made on these drop roll machines since 1891.

335
00:20:13,360 --> 00:20:16,560
And of course, that's why they're called drops.

336
00:20:16,660 --> 00:20:19,900
Red Hot Hearts are all the rage in February.

337
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,260
But in December, they can't keep the candy canes,

338
00:20:22,261 --> 00:20:25,500
and hard-candied fish on the shelf.

339
00:20:25,600 --> 00:20:28,460
The fish are given their pearly look from a good old-fashioned

340
00:20:28,560 --> 00:20:34,000
hand pulling, courtesy of Warren, and it's one sweet workout.

341
00:20:34,100 --> 00:20:37,500
They also make a ribbon candy.

342
00:20:37,600 --> 00:20:40,580
This nostalgic candy is fashioned the really old-fashioned

343
00:20:40,680 --> 00:20:47,080
way, on a hand-crank machine that requires more than a few hands.

344
00:20:47,081 --> 00:20:55,080
hands. The Shimps take real pride in the hands-on technique

345
00:20:55,081 --> 00:21:00,120
employed in their candy kitchen, step into the adjacent museum,

346
00:21:00,121 --> 00:21:04,040
and take a glimpse back in time. Their collection includes

347
00:21:04,041 --> 00:21:07,040
more than 3,000 pieces of candy-making machinery and

348
00:21:07,041 --> 00:21:11,080
memorabilia dating back to the 1800s.

349
00:21:11,081 --> 00:21:14,080
I'm trying to preserve the history of an industry here,

350
00:21:14,081 --> 00:21:17,580
of an industry here, and it's bigger than just shims itself.

351
00:21:17,581 --> 00:21:19,960
What I've got here is a fantastic collection

352
00:21:19,961 --> 00:21:22,420
of the history of the candy industry in this country.

353
00:21:22,421 --> 00:21:24,120
All the companies that have come and gone,

354
00:21:24,121 --> 00:21:26,920
I mean, you see the name of a company that's been around,

355
00:21:26,921 --> 00:21:29,160
and you say, why did that company go out of business?

356
00:21:29,161 --> 00:21:31,100
You know, what products did they make?

357
00:21:31,101 --> 00:21:34,940
Where did they, you know, get the raw materials from?

358
00:21:34,941 --> 00:21:36,700
Good question.

359
00:21:36,701 --> 00:21:39,280
So where do today's large candy manufacturers

360
00:21:39,281 --> 00:21:41,580
get the raw materials for things like dirt

361
00:21:41,581 --> 00:21:44,080
and vomit-flavored candy?

362
00:21:44,081 --> 00:21:48,080
Jim's confectionery produces about 8,000 pounds of Red Hots

363
00:21:48,081 --> 00:21:49,080
per year.

364
00:21:49,081 --> 00:21:53,080
A large-scale candy manufacturer can make almost twice that

365
00:21:53,081 --> 00:21:55,080
amount of Red Hots in one hour.

366
00:22:07,080 --> 00:22:11,080
In 2005, more than 40 billion jelly beans were eaten

367
00:22:11,081 --> 00:22:12,080
worldwide.

368
00:22:14,080 --> 00:22:16,480
Strung end to end, that's almost enough to reach to the moon

369
00:22:16,580 --> 00:22:18,180
and back.

370
00:22:18,280 --> 00:22:19,680
The origin of jelly beans goes back

371
00:22:19,780 --> 00:22:23,560
to pre-biblical times when a confection known as Turkish

372
00:22:23,660 --> 00:22:28,900
Delights had surfaced, and it was a jelly confection.

373
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:32,460
Turkish Delight is a soft jelly center made primarily

374
00:22:32,560 --> 00:22:34,960
from sugar, cornstarch, and flavors,

375
00:22:35,060 --> 00:22:38,240
cooked to about 240 degrees Fahrenheit,

376
00:22:38,340 --> 00:22:43,340
with a powdered sugar exterior that could get pretty messy.

377
00:22:43,341 --> 00:22:46,980
But a process called panning, introduced in 17th century

378
00:22:47,080 --> 00:22:50,080
France and still in use today, enabled candy makers

379
00:22:50,180 --> 00:22:53,280
to coat candy in nuts in a hard shell by rolling them

380
00:22:53,380 --> 00:22:56,820
in sugar and syrup. A soft jelly center

381
00:22:56,920 --> 00:23:01,760
and a hard candy shell were a natural match.

382
00:23:01,860 --> 00:23:05,000
The jelly bean was introduced in the late 1800s,

383
00:23:05,100 --> 00:23:09,040
but it didn't grab the spotlight until decades later.

384
00:23:09,140 --> 00:23:11,700
Jelly beans became connected with Easter,

385
00:23:11,701 --> 00:23:14,340
And they were sort of thought of as small eggs,

386
00:23:14,440 --> 00:23:16,580
and they were given as Easter presents.

387
00:23:16,680 --> 00:23:18,780
Once the standard fruit flavored beans

388
00:23:18,880 --> 00:23:21,780
became mainstream, there wasn't much else that could be done

389
00:23:21,880 --> 00:23:23,620
to improve the candy.

390
00:23:23,720 --> 00:23:24,680
Wrong.

391
00:23:24,780 --> 00:23:27,480
Enter the Jelly Belly.

392
00:23:27,580 --> 00:23:30,320
Jelly beans are made with sugar, corn syrup,

393
00:23:30,420 --> 00:23:32,220
and corn starch, as is Jelly Belly.

394
00:23:32,320 --> 00:23:35,360
But the unique differences between a standard jelly bean

395
00:23:35,460 --> 00:23:38,420
and Jelly Belly jelly beans, it's all in the process.

396
00:23:38,520 --> 00:23:41,500
We flavor the center gel and the jelly

397
00:23:41,501 --> 00:23:43,500
with true flavors whenever possible.

398
00:23:46,500 --> 00:23:50,500
Created in 1976 with just eight experimental flavors,

399
00:23:50,501 --> 00:23:54,500
today Jelly Belly boasts over 50 not-so-standard varieties.

400
00:23:57,500 --> 00:24:00,500
The 200,000-square-foot Jelly Belly production facility

401
00:24:00,501 --> 00:24:06,500
in Fairfield, California can produce 80 million beans per day.

402
00:24:06,501 --> 00:24:08,500
All the ingredients go into the whey vessel.

403
00:24:08,501 --> 00:24:10,500
That's the common denominator of everything that we use.

404
00:24:10,501 --> 00:24:11,740
that we use up here.

405
00:24:11,840 --> 00:24:13,800
Then it's stirred.

406
00:24:13,900 --> 00:24:15,900
Then it's dropped into a warm-mix kettle.

407
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:20,040
In the warm-mix kettle, it's heated to 75.5 degrees Celsius,

408
00:24:20,140 --> 00:24:22,540
and it stays in there until we get ready to cook it

409
00:24:22,640 --> 00:24:24,740
and send it over to the CFM kettle.

410
00:24:24,840 --> 00:24:27,620
And the CFM kettle is where we add the color and the flavor

411
00:24:27,720 --> 00:24:28,520
mix.

412
00:24:28,620 --> 00:24:31,060
All of these have their own secret recipe,

413
00:24:31,160 --> 00:24:33,160
and I don't even know the ingredients that actually

414
00:24:33,260 --> 00:24:34,160
go into all of them.

415
00:24:36,660 --> 00:24:39,260
Each batch is tested before it's sent downstairs

416
00:24:39,261 --> 00:24:42,260
to the Mogul, a giant contraption that does more

417
00:24:42,360 --> 00:24:44,600
than its fair share of the jelly belly making work.

418
00:24:47,860 --> 00:24:50,800
The hot liquid candy mixture travels through a pipe

419
00:24:50,900 --> 00:24:54,500
and is deposited into 30 trays each minute.

420
00:24:54,600 --> 00:24:58,620
Every tray bears 1,260 impressions of jelly beans

421
00:24:58,720 --> 00:25:01,420
in cornstarch.

422
00:25:01,520 --> 00:25:04,920
The trays are stacked and put into a dry room, where the candy

423
00:25:05,020 --> 00:25:09,260
will solidify for 24 to 48 hours.

424
00:25:09,261 --> 00:25:11,960
The beans are then dumped out of the cornstarch trays

425
00:25:12,060 --> 00:25:15,200
and sent through a sugar sander, where they're coated in sugar.

426
00:25:19,040 --> 00:25:21,900
This initial sugar layer acts like a primer coat

427
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:25,380
and prevents the jelly belly centers from sticking together.

428
00:25:25,480 --> 00:25:28,380
And this is what the product looks like after it comes out

429
00:25:28,480 --> 00:25:29,880
of the sugar sander.

430
00:25:29,980 --> 00:25:33,920
And it's ready to go to engrossing.

431
00:25:34,020 --> 00:25:37,320
Engrossing is the industrial term for the centuries-old

432
00:25:37,420 --> 00:25:39,260
panning process.

433
00:25:39,261 --> 00:25:43,260
where candy centers receive their hard candy shells.

434
00:25:43,360 --> 00:25:46,760
It takes about two hours to engross these things.

435
00:25:46,860 --> 00:25:48,700
First thing, you put the syrup on,

436
00:25:48,800 --> 00:25:51,000
and it gets all sticky and gooey in there.

437
00:25:51,100 --> 00:25:53,300
And then the pan men put on sugar.

438
00:25:53,400 --> 00:25:55,880
And they do that four different times.

439
00:25:55,980 --> 00:25:59,320
And this is a really incredible piece of art form

440
00:25:59,420 --> 00:26:01,620
of making candy.

441
00:26:01,720 --> 00:26:03,320
The nearly completed jelly bellies

442
00:26:03,420 --> 00:26:06,520
are then placed into giant stainless steel drums

443
00:26:06,521 --> 00:26:10,220
that hold about 500 pounds each.

444
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:13,020
Here, the candy will get its top coat.

445
00:26:13,120 --> 00:26:15,720
And we first put on a powder that allows the candy

446
00:26:15,820 --> 00:26:17,660
to run real smooth.

447
00:26:17,760 --> 00:26:20,160
And then we use confectioner's glaze,

448
00:26:20,260 --> 00:26:21,760
and we use beeswax.

449
00:26:21,860 --> 00:26:25,780
And those are all FDA-approved products that actually does

450
00:26:25,880 --> 00:26:26,680
the sealing.

451
00:26:26,780 --> 00:26:29,480
It's what gives the beautiful sheen on the product.

452
00:26:32,580 --> 00:26:35,820
Next, inspectors scrutinize the beans, the ones that are

453
00:26:35,821 --> 00:26:41,720
that are rejected become belly flops sold in factory stores.

454
00:26:41,820 --> 00:26:44,620
The perfect beans travel through pipes onto a tray

455
00:26:44,720 --> 00:26:47,060
with individual pockets.

456
00:26:47,160 --> 00:26:50,360
Snug in their slots, they pass under a roller that prints

457
00:26:50,460 --> 00:26:54,400
in white food coloring the unmistakable Jelly Belly name

458
00:26:54,500 --> 00:26:57,780
on 20,700 beans per minute.

459
00:26:57,880 --> 00:27:00,280
Robots have taken over a lot of the backbreaking work

460
00:27:00,380 --> 00:27:01,580
of yesteryear.

461
00:27:01,680 --> 00:27:03,880
In the 60s, when we started making jelly beans,

462
00:27:03,980 --> 00:27:05,420
it was all by hand.

463
00:27:05,421 --> 00:27:08,420
We probably lifted 16,000 pounds a day.

464
00:27:08,421 --> 00:27:11,420
Today, a dream has come true.

465
00:27:11,421 --> 00:27:14,420
We've been able to use a lot of robots to save the back.

466
00:27:14,421 --> 00:27:18,420
And I sure wish I had it when I was there stacking those trays.

467
00:27:18,421 --> 00:27:25,420
But today, we stack probably about 60,000 pounds a shift with this particular robot.

468
00:27:25,421 --> 00:27:29,420
Servo motors are the heart of a robot.

469
00:27:29,421 --> 00:27:32,420
A servo motor is run by a computer.

470
00:27:32,421 --> 00:27:34,280
And it can go an inch.

471
00:27:34,281 --> 00:27:35,320
It can go, ooh.

472
00:27:35,321 --> 00:27:37,920
It can go real fast and slow down and stop.

473
00:27:37,921 --> 00:27:39,560
It can shake a little bit.

474
00:27:39,561 --> 00:27:42,900
It can go up and down.

475
00:27:42,901 --> 00:27:44,460
These robots are so efficient.

476
00:27:44,461 --> 00:27:47,560
They can work down to within an eighth of an inch.

477
00:27:47,561 --> 00:27:50,800
And they can lift thousands of pounds.

478
00:27:50,801 --> 00:27:54,040
And everybody loves them.

479
00:27:54,041 --> 00:27:56,280
What Jelly Belly consumers really love

480
00:27:56,281 --> 00:28:00,540
is the variety of Jelly Belly flavors.

481
00:28:00,541 --> 00:28:02,700
From the savory and fruity...

482
00:28:02,701 --> 00:28:03,740
You like the worms?

483
00:28:03,741 --> 00:28:04,580
I like it.

484
00:28:04,581 --> 00:28:07,220
...to the downright disgusting...

485
00:28:07,221 --> 00:28:10,140
When we go to create the flavors, like with peach,

486
00:28:10,141 --> 00:28:14,060
we go buy peaches that are just right, just right.

487
00:28:14,061 --> 00:28:18,060
And we make the candy with the flavor and with the peach puree,

488
00:28:18,061 --> 00:28:19,500
and then we taste them.

489
00:28:19,501 --> 00:28:21,820
And we taste the peach, and we taste the candy.

490
00:28:21,821 --> 00:28:24,700
And we taste the peach, and we taste the candy.

491
00:28:24,701 --> 00:28:26,660
Watermelon is a wonderful flavor.

492
00:28:26,661 --> 00:28:29,140
And of course, watermelon is red on the inside

493
00:28:29,141 --> 00:28:32,100
and green on the outside.

494
00:28:32,101 --> 00:28:34,980
There are some flavors here that I lost a lot of sleep over.

495
00:28:34,981 --> 00:28:36,680
And one of them is vomit.

496
00:28:36,681 --> 00:28:38,640
How do you know what vomit tastes like?

497
00:28:38,641 --> 00:28:41,180
Well, we had produced a pepperoni pizza about three

498
00:28:41,181 --> 00:28:43,080
or four years prior to that.

499
00:28:43,081 --> 00:28:45,620
And one of our staff said, Herm, you remember how bad

500
00:28:45,621 --> 00:28:46,860
that pepperoni pizza was?

501
00:28:46,861 --> 00:28:48,360
You could only eat one piece, and you almost

502
00:28:48,361 --> 00:28:50,000
had to go throw up.

503
00:28:50,001 --> 00:28:53,000
Well, we went back and made that formula again,

504
00:28:53,001 --> 00:28:55,800
and it was bad because of the cheese and stuff

505
00:28:55,801 --> 00:28:57,460
in the pepperoni pizza.

506
00:28:57,461 --> 00:29:00,360
But it wasn't quite bad enough, so we made another batch

507
00:29:00,361 --> 00:29:02,740
with some citric acid, because when you throw up,

508
00:29:02,741 --> 00:29:04,740
you want to have a little bit of acid in there, too.

509
00:29:06,840 --> 00:29:08,020
Vomit not your thing?

510
00:29:08,660 --> 00:29:12,560
They also make soap, earthworm, rotten egg, and booger flavors.

511
00:29:14,300 --> 00:29:16,000
All fit for a wizard.

512
00:29:16,820 --> 00:29:19,240
People say, Herm, how can you do this?

513
00:29:19,780 --> 00:29:23,140
I have never seen anybody that I have shown this to

514
00:29:23,141 --> 00:29:25,360
that hasn't gotten a big, huge smile on it

515
00:29:25,361 --> 00:29:26,920
and say, how terrible, that's awful.

516
00:29:26,921 --> 00:29:29,920
It continues to sell and sell and sell.

517
00:29:31,920 --> 00:29:34,920
Hard on the outside, soft on the inside,

518
00:29:34,921 --> 00:29:37,920
Jelly Bellies are a sweet success.

519
00:29:37,921 --> 00:29:40,920
But if you're into some really soft candy that comes in a

520
00:29:40,921 --> 00:29:43,920
rainbow of flavors, try some saltwater taffy.

521
00:29:43,921 --> 00:29:47,920
And no, there isn't any saltwater in it.

522
00:29:47,921 --> 00:29:50,920
Saltwater taffy has kind of an interesting history.

523
00:29:50,921 --> 00:29:54,920
This taffy was offered at resorts, particularly in Atlantic

524
00:29:54,921 --> 00:29:58,860
in Atlantic City, there was a storm came once,

525
00:29:58,960 --> 00:30:01,160
and a store was flooded.

526
00:30:01,260 --> 00:30:04,160
And a person came into the store right after the storm

527
00:30:04,260 --> 00:30:06,360
and said, do you still have any taffy?

528
00:30:06,460 --> 00:30:10,140
And the owner said, yes, but I think it's now saltwater taffy.

529
00:30:10,240 --> 00:30:12,540
So whether that's true or not, we're not sure.

530
00:30:12,640 --> 00:30:17,440
But that is one story about the history of taffy.

531
00:30:17,540 --> 00:30:20,740
Carousel Candies in Monterey, California,

532
00:30:20,840 --> 00:30:24,120
offers, among other things, an array

533
00:30:24,121 --> 00:30:28,600
colorful, flavorful taffy made right on the premises.

534
00:30:28,660 --> 00:30:30,860
The ingredients that go into saltwater taffy

535
00:30:30,920 --> 00:30:34,600
is corn syrup, sugar.

536
00:30:34,660 --> 00:30:38,940
It has a small amount of fat that's used as a release,

537
00:30:39,000 --> 00:30:42,500
so it doesn't stick.

538
00:30:42,580 --> 00:30:45,280
The concoction is cooked to 240 degrees

539
00:30:45,340 --> 00:30:48,680
for 30 minutes and then spread on a water-cooled table.

540
00:30:48,740 --> 00:30:50,840
We are making peppermint taffy.

541
00:30:50,920 --> 00:30:54,120
We colored some of the taffy for the stripes.

542
00:30:54,121 --> 00:30:56,620
Now I've taken the remainder of the batch,

543
00:30:56,720 --> 00:31:01,160
and I'm going to pull it on the puller here.

544
00:31:01,260 --> 00:31:03,800
That's where it gets its aeration and elasticity.

545
00:31:03,900 --> 00:31:06,240
Also, we incorporate the flavoring.

546
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:16,840
Momentum keeps the candy on the puller.

547
00:31:16,940 --> 00:31:20,080
With every rotation, layers of air are incorporated,

548
00:31:20,180 --> 00:31:24,120
and the candy becomes whiter and softer.

549
00:31:24,121 --> 00:31:28,120
Friction heats the candy to prepare it to receive the flavoring.

550
00:31:28,220 --> 00:31:32,600
After 10 to 15 minutes, the candy is ready to come off.

551
00:31:32,700 --> 00:31:37,160
And then we'll bring it back to the table to cool it

552
00:31:37,260 --> 00:31:39,240
and give it its coloring.

553
00:31:39,340 --> 00:31:43,880
Cooked, pulled, flavored, colored, and cooled,

554
00:31:43,980 --> 00:31:46,940
the taffy is ready to get its signature red and white striped

555
00:31:47,040 --> 00:31:48,940
peppermint appearance.

556
00:31:49,040 --> 00:31:51,740
Forming the candy right now, just shaping it.

557
00:31:51,741 --> 00:31:53,240
Now we're gonna add our stripes.

558
00:31:56,740 --> 00:31:58,740
Then it's off to the batch roller,

559
00:31:58,840 --> 00:32:02,380
where the taffy is kneaded and elongated,

560
00:32:02,480 --> 00:32:05,980
on its way to the extrusion head,

561
00:32:06,080 --> 00:32:08,320
and ultimately, the cutter and wrapper.

562
00:32:13,800 --> 00:32:16,740
All of this is done on one very old machine.

563
00:32:16,840 --> 00:32:19,100
The equipment is hard to find.

564
00:32:19,101 --> 00:32:22,440
The equipment we have is 60 to 65 years old.

565
00:32:22,500 --> 00:32:25,840
You can't really find parts for this type of equipment.

566
00:32:25,900 --> 00:32:31,020
Sometimes we have to have our parts made by a machinist.

567
00:32:31,080 --> 00:32:32,580
Taffy, especially.

568
00:32:32,640 --> 00:32:35,280
You don't see too many people making taffy.

569
00:32:35,340 --> 00:32:39,220
Candy making is a dying art.

570
00:32:39,280 --> 00:32:42,860
Maybe, but some of the old brands are very much alive.

571
00:32:42,920 --> 00:32:46,860
People still go nuts over some really twisted favorites.

572
00:32:49,100 --> 00:32:52,940
In 1983, President Ronald Reagan had Jelly Belly Jelly

573
00:32:52,941 --> 00:32:56,440
Bean stowed on the space shuttle Challenger as a surprise for

574
00:32:56,441 --> 00:32:57,340
the astronauts.

575
00:33:05,340 --> 00:33:08,960
Candy can evolve at lightning speed based on the ever

576
00:33:08,961 --> 00:33:11,760
changing tastes of the fickle consumer.

577
00:33:11,761 --> 00:33:14,260
Still, there are some candies that haven't changed all that

578
00:33:14,261 --> 00:33:16,960
much in many years.

579
00:33:16,961 --> 00:33:19,600
Take black licorice, for example.

580
00:33:19,700 --> 00:33:23,200
Just your simple, basic root.

581
00:33:23,300 --> 00:33:27,500
Archeologists found licorice sticks in King Tut's tomb,

582
00:33:27,600 --> 00:33:29,700
and it was prescribed as a cure-all as far back

583
00:33:29,800 --> 00:33:31,800
as 5,000 years ago.

584
00:33:31,900 --> 00:33:34,720
Sages recognize the healing properties of licorice.

585
00:33:34,820 --> 00:33:36,820
And because of its sweet characteristic,

586
00:33:36,920 --> 00:33:40,720
they were able to mix this with other herbs to create remedies.

587
00:33:40,820 --> 00:33:43,920
And in fact, Alexander the Great and the Roman emperor

588
00:33:43,921 --> 00:33:48,520
used licorice for its many healing properties.

589
00:33:48,620 --> 00:33:51,520
Though long believed to be an anti-inflammatory

590
00:33:51,620 --> 00:33:54,460
and soothing agent, licorice didn't become a candy

591
00:33:54,560 --> 00:33:58,660
until sugar and flour were added to it in 1760.

592
00:33:58,760 --> 00:34:01,500
Licorice candy was traditionally black in color

593
00:34:01,600 --> 00:34:04,740
because the extract is dark brown.

594
00:34:04,840 --> 00:34:07,780
Today, licorice is an all-encompassing term,

595
00:34:07,880 --> 00:34:11,580
referring to candy that has a particular shape and texture.

596
00:34:11,581 --> 00:34:16,060
whether or not there is any licorice extract in it.

597
00:34:16,160 --> 00:34:18,720
The most common color is red.

598
00:34:18,820 --> 00:34:22,360
The American Licorice Company of Union City, California,

599
00:34:22,460 --> 00:34:26,720
has been making this stuff since 1914.

600
00:34:26,820 --> 00:34:31,700
They produce 200,000 pounds of candy every day.

601
00:34:31,800 --> 00:34:35,700
That's 1,200 miles of licorice roll.

602
00:34:35,800 --> 00:34:37,700
Corn syrup is our first ingredient

603
00:34:37,800 --> 00:34:39,340
being added to the batch.

604
00:34:39,341 --> 00:34:41,740
Next, we'll be putting some wheat flour in,

605
00:34:41,840 --> 00:34:45,620
then some high fructose corn sweetener, then some water.

606
00:34:45,720 --> 00:34:50,620
That'll be followed by citric acid and red 40, the coloring.

607
00:34:50,720 --> 00:34:52,620
The flour will act as a thickener

608
00:34:52,720 --> 00:34:56,820
that'll transform the product from a liquid state into a dough.

609
00:34:56,920 --> 00:34:59,700
At the end of the cooking period, which is about two hours,

610
00:34:59,800 --> 00:35:01,300
we'll put the flavor in.

611
00:35:01,400 --> 00:35:03,460
The reason we put the flavor in at the end

612
00:35:03,560 --> 00:35:06,140
is so it won't flash off into the atmosphere,

613
00:35:06,240 --> 00:35:09,340
and it'll stay in the red vine's candy.

614
00:35:09,341 --> 00:35:13,240
Each cattle holds about 4,200 pounds of red vines dough

615
00:35:13,340 --> 00:35:14,480
candy.

616
00:35:14,580 --> 00:35:19,080
We empty it into these dough cans and let it sit overnight,

617
00:35:19,180 --> 00:35:22,280
and let it cool before we put it into our extruders.

618
00:35:22,380 --> 00:35:25,720
Before it goes in, we give it a little slap to make sure it's

619
00:35:25,820 --> 00:35:29,600
the right consistency to go into the extruder.

620
00:35:29,700 --> 00:35:34,000
Each dough can holds 350 pounds of candy, which will

621
00:35:34,100 --> 00:35:37,000
yield about 5,000 pieces of red vines.

622
00:35:37,001 --> 00:35:40,700
The dough is sent down a chute to the extruder.

623
00:35:40,800 --> 00:35:43,700
This is where the candy gets its famous appearance,

624
00:35:43,800 --> 00:35:45,740
twisted and tubular.

625
00:35:45,840 --> 00:35:49,220
The nozzles on this press put their characteristic spin

626
00:35:49,320 --> 00:35:52,240
on the red vine, and we put a little touch of air

627
00:35:52,340 --> 00:35:56,120
through the middle of it to blow it up a little bit.

628
00:35:56,220 --> 00:36:03,320
Then it gets cut and racks, and then put in our dry rooms.

629
00:36:03,420 --> 00:36:07,000
Five hours in the dry room at 160 feet.

630
00:36:07,001 --> 00:36:09,000
and the rack of candy is off for packaging.

631
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:16,000
This is where we package our Red Vine tray candy.

632
00:36:16,001 --> 00:36:19,000
We pack about 75 of these every minute.

633
00:36:22,000 --> 00:36:26,000
About six miles away in Hayward, California,

634
00:36:26,001 --> 00:36:30,000
the Annabelle Candy Company packs about 150 nougatty Big Hunk

635
00:36:30,001 --> 00:36:32,000
bars per minute.

636
00:36:32,001 --> 00:36:34,000
Like most candy products, the Big Hunk

637
00:36:34,001 --> 00:36:36,000
begins life in the kettle.

638
00:36:36,001 --> 00:36:40,000
Sugar, corn syrup, honey, and water are cooked for about a

639
00:36:40,100 --> 00:36:43,800
half an hour to just under 300 degrees.

640
00:36:43,900 --> 00:36:47,740
After it's mixed in the kettles, it's emptied into a

641
00:36:47,840 --> 00:36:51,340
Hobart mixer, which is a giant kitchen-like blender, but it's

642
00:36:51,440 --> 00:36:52,760
much bigger.

643
00:36:52,860 --> 00:36:57,220
And we add into it vegetable oil, peanuts, and natural and

644
00:36:57,320 --> 00:36:58,720
artificial flavors.

645
00:36:58,820 --> 00:37:00,420
And I can't tell you exactly what those are.

646
00:37:00,520 --> 00:37:03,320
Those are sort of secret ingredients.

647
00:37:03,321 --> 00:37:08,060
The bucket of thick, sticky stuff is then elevated to a hopper

648
00:37:08,160 --> 00:37:12,060
that extrudes it into a long, flat pan that'll go into the

649
00:37:12,160 --> 00:37:14,560
oven, where it will stay overnight.

650
00:37:17,160 --> 00:37:20,900
Then the pans are emptied and sized so that they are the

651
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:24,140
exact shape of a big hunk bar, and they are put through a

652
00:37:24,240 --> 00:37:27,420
cutter, which uses an ultrasound cutting mechanism in order to

653
00:37:27,520 --> 00:37:29,980
slice them smoothly.

654
00:37:30,080 --> 00:37:32,520
Wrapped and packaged, these puppies are ready for

655
00:37:32,521 --> 00:37:36,820
are ready for eating.

656
00:37:36,920 --> 00:37:39,560
Except for the automation, Annabelle's candy making

657
00:37:39,660 --> 00:37:43,760
process and products are essentially unchanged.

658
00:37:43,860 --> 00:37:46,960
It's the packaging that has revolutionized the industry.

659
00:37:47,060 --> 00:37:50,040
For one thing, we used to fold the wrappers around the candy

660
00:37:50,140 --> 00:37:52,880
bars, and I actually have an old one right here.

661
00:37:52,980 --> 00:37:56,180
You can see how the bar, the end is folded.

662
00:37:56,280 --> 00:37:58,380
Here's how we do the new ones.

663
00:37:58,480 --> 00:38:02,220
They're cold sealed, so they're crimped at the ends.

664
00:38:02,221 --> 00:38:04,220
And this provides an excellent oxygen barrier,

665
00:38:04,320 --> 00:38:07,020
as well as the new materials that they've come up with,

666
00:38:07,120 --> 00:38:09,520
which also enhance the oxygen barrier so that the candy

667
00:38:09,620 --> 00:38:10,320
doesn't get stale.

668
00:38:13,040 --> 00:38:15,300
One of Annabelle's most recognizable wrappers

669
00:38:15,400 --> 00:38:19,200
belongs to the Aba Zaba, a slightly more complicated

670
00:38:19,300 --> 00:38:24,680
product, a taffy candy with a peanut butter center.

671
00:38:24,780 --> 00:38:26,080
But how do they get it in there?

672
00:38:28,840 --> 00:38:30,440
First, they make the taffy.

673
00:38:30,441 --> 00:38:33,540
But unlike traditional taffy, this candy is stored overnight

674
00:38:33,640 --> 00:38:35,540
in a heating room at 120 degrees.

675
00:38:39,840 --> 00:38:43,060
We're going to be whipping over here for about five minutes

676
00:38:43,160 --> 00:38:44,760
until it changes color.

677
00:38:44,860 --> 00:38:47,220
Automatically, you're going to change the color

678
00:38:47,320 --> 00:38:49,320
from gold to white.

679
00:38:49,420 --> 00:38:54,960
After this is done, we're going to the horse machine.

680
00:38:55,060 --> 00:38:57,640
The horse is where the taffy is flattened,

681
00:38:57,740 --> 00:39:00,440
ready to receive its nutty center.

682
00:39:00,441 --> 00:39:04,280
This is the way how we put the peanut butter inside to the Abba Zaba.

683
00:39:05,240 --> 00:39:09,520
To go through the knife over here to make it the regular bars.

684
00:39:09,800 --> 00:39:11,620
It's going a long way this tunnel.

685
00:39:14,880 --> 00:39:16,800
This is the knife in here.

686
00:39:17,100 --> 00:39:24,560
We roll it out and cut in pieces to go directly to the wrapper machine over there on the other side.

687
00:39:24,561 --> 00:39:28,560
While some companies like Annabelle Candy and the American

688
00:39:28,660 --> 00:39:32,360
Licorice Company stick with what works, others have gone

689
00:39:32,460 --> 00:39:37,500
completely buggy, looking for a taste you can't resist.

690
00:39:41,580 --> 00:39:45,680
In 1925, the American Licorice Company made licorice shoes for

691
00:39:45,780 --> 00:39:50,580
Charlie Chaplin to eat in the film The Gold Rush.

692
00:39:54,560 --> 00:39:59,560
While kids may be the most conspicuous consumers

693
00:39:59,660 --> 00:40:04,060
of conventional candy, adults need their sugar rushes too.

694
00:40:04,160 --> 00:40:07,800
Gourmet candy is an emerging market geared toward the

695
00:40:07,900 --> 00:40:11,200
pickiest of palates. And at high-end chocolatiers

696
00:40:11,300 --> 00:40:16,320
like Joseph Schmidt and Sharpen Burger, only the very

697
00:40:16,420 --> 00:40:20,860
finest ingredients and cocoa beans go into their gourmet

698
00:40:20,861 --> 00:40:23,820
go into their gourmet chocolate and handmade creations.

699
00:40:25,500 --> 00:40:28,600
But sometimes it's what's on the outside that counts, too.

700
00:40:29,800 --> 00:40:34,000
One of the reasons that our product is so sought after

701
00:40:34,001 --> 00:40:35,600
is our packaging.

702
00:40:36,540 --> 00:40:38,440
When you look at our chocolate,

703
00:40:38,600 --> 00:40:40,980
you see the package before you see the chocolate.

704
00:40:42,100 --> 00:40:46,520
Most of our boxes are hand-painted, hand-crafted,

705
00:40:46,521 --> 00:40:50,260
made out of wood or paper, cardboard,

706
00:40:50,261 --> 00:40:54,460
rice paper and some other material.

707
00:40:54,560 --> 00:40:58,460
Holiday time or any time, a customer at Joseph Schmidt

708
00:40:58,560 --> 00:41:01,800
can choose his pleasure from a stunning array of perfectly

709
00:41:01,900 --> 00:41:06,640
prepared, beautifully decorated and packaged confections.

710
00:41:06,740 --> 00:41:10,640
But buyer beware, these sinful treats don't come cheap.

711
00:41:10,740 --> 00:41:14,120
Expect to pay at least $15 for a 12-piece box.

712
00:41:16,620 --> 00:41:20,220
For the discerning hard candy fan, hot candy

713
00:41:20,260 --> 00:41:26,260
makes treats using only the finest in invertebrates.

714
00:41:26,360 --> 00:41:29,700
This isn't your grandma's candy.

715
00:41:29,800 --> 00:41:33,580
Worm candy is a lollipop with a real worm inside.

716
00:41:33,680 --> 00:41:35,780
Mmm.

717
00:41:35,880 --> 00:41:40,740
Cricket Lick It is much the same, made with real crickets.

718
00:41:40,840 --> 00:41:44,660
Amber Insect Inside looks like fossilized amber,

719
00:41:44,760 --> 00:41:48,780
but is actually toffee-flavored candy with real insects and plant

720
00:41:48,781 --> 00:41:51,920
and plant life embedded in it.

721
00:41:52,020 --> 00:41:53,920
And no such store would be complete

722
00:41:54,020 --> 00:41:57,280
without chocolate-covered creatures.

723
00:41:57,380 --> 00:41:59,320
But who would actually eat this stuff?

724
00:41:59,420 --> 00:42:01,220
Mmm.

725
00:42:01,320 --> 00:42:02,620
That's a mouthful.

726
00:42:02,720 --> 00:42:05,120
Some of our best customers are women.

727
00:42:05,220 --> 00:42:09,440
You know, they buy the candy for their husbands for a joke.

728
00:42:09,540 --> 00:42:12,040
But then the kids, the kids really like them.

729
00:42:12,140 --> 00:42:13,640
And they say, look, I ate a worm.

730
00:42:13,740 --> 00:42:15,680
You know, a little bragging right type thing.

731
00:42:15,780 --> 00:42:17,680
Ooh, this one has a cricket in it.

732
00:42:17,780 --> 00:42:18,780
Ew.

733
00:42:18,781 --> 00:42:23,780
So when somebody eats a scorpion, that's a mark of macho.

734
00:42:23,781 --> 00:42:28,780
The real question is, how do they get the critter inside the candy?

735
00:42:28,781 --> 00:42:32,780
These are the worms we use in our candy-making process.

736
00:42:32,781 --> 00:42:35,780
They're grown on good, fresh grain,

737
00:42:35,781 --> 00:42:38,780
and things like apples and other fresh fruits.

738
00:42:38,781 --> 00:42:40,780
So we know they're nutritious.

739
00:42:40,781 --> 00:42:42,780
We know they're pesticide-free,

740
00:42:42,781 --> 00:42:44,780
and they're ready for human consumption.

741
00:42:44,781 --> 00:42:47,780
We put them in like a state of semi-hibernation.

742
00:42:47,781 --> 00:42:49,780
We take them down to a very cool temperature.

743
00:42:49,880 --> 00:42:52,660
We then bake them until they're crispy

744
00:42:52,760 --> 00:42:54,760
and ready to go into the product.

745
00:42:54,860 --> 00:42:58,360
The processing of scorpions has an extra little step in it.

746
00:42:58,460 --> 00:43:02,200
After the hibernation process, we boil it in tequila.

747
00:43:02,300 --> 00:43:04,300
And this gives it not only a preservative,

748
00:43:04,400 --> 00:43:06,460
but makes the scorpion more pliable

749
00:43:06,560 --> 00:43:08,460
for when it's ready to go into the sucker.

750
00:43:08,560 --> 00:43:10,000
Then we go ahead and bake it.

751
00:43:10,100 --> 00:43:14,600
So the scorpion requires a little extra care.

752
00:43:14,700 --> 00:43:17,380
Next, workers make a hard candy mixture

753
00:43:17,381 --> 00:43:20,720
in the standard copper kettle.

754
00:43:20,820 --> 00:43:24,820
The candy is poured into molds on a cool marble table.

755
00:43:24,920 --> 00:43:29,120
Then a scorpion is gently placed in the center.

756
00:43:29,220 --> 00:43:30,660
Throw in the lollipop stick.

757
00:43:34,160 --> 00:43:38,660
And wait five minutes for it to completely cool and harden.

758
00:43:38,760 --> 00:43:42,400
Voila, a candy with an unusual crunch that's

759
